  <h1>Common Elder Abuse Claim Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Families dealing with elder abuse and nursing home neglect frequently arrive with a set of assumptions about how the legal process works &mdash; many of which are wrong. This guide walks through fifteen of the most common misconceptions, with what the actual rules say and what they mean practically.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 1: "If I sign that arbitration clause, the lawsuit is over before it starts."</h2>

  <p>This is the most important and most under-appreciated issue in nursing home cases. Many admission contracts contain pre-dispute arbitration clauses, but enforceability is not automatic. Real defenses include (a) the signer lacked authority (a family member signed but didn&rsquo;t actually have power of attorney); (b) the resident lacked capacity at signing; (c) the clause is procedurally or substantively unconscionable under the controlling state law; (d) state-specific limits on mandatory arbitration for nursing home residents on Medicaid; and (e) federal CMS regulatory limits that vary over time. The first thing an experienced elder abuse attorney does is analyze the arbitration clause &mdash; do not assume it&rsquo;s a closed door.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 2: "There&rsquo;s no big damages because elderly people don&rsquo;t have much lost income."</h2>

  <p>Lost income is a small piece of elder abuse damages. The dominant categories are pain and suffering (often the largest), medical expenses, wrongful death damages, and &mdash; in states with strong Elder Abuse Acts like California &mdash; statutory enhancements (attorney fees, treble damages, recovery of pre-death pain and suffering by heirs). A serious nursing home neglect case can recover well into seven figures despite zero lost income.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 3: "If APS didn&rsquo;t substantiate the report, the case is dead."</h2>

  <p>An unsubstantiated APS finding makes a civil case harder, but not impossible. APS investigations have time and resource constraints, work to a different evidentiary standard than civil litigation, and sometimes miss what an experienced records review picks up. Several substantial cases have been litigated successfully despite APS not substantiating the underlying conduct &mdash; particularly where additional evidence (CMS surveys, internal facility documents, employee witnesses) emerges in discovery.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 4: "F-Tag deficiencies don&rsquo;t matter unless they relate to my specific situation."</h2>

  <p>F-Tag deficiencies establish notice and pattern. A facility with a prior F-Tag F686 (pressure ulcers) deficiency that subsequently allows another resident to develop a Stage IV ulcer has been put on notice that its pressure-ulcer-prevention practices are deficient. That notice is critical to establishing the heightened-conduct showing (recklessness, oppression, fraud, or malice) needed for statutory enhancements. Even seemingly unrelated F-Tags can establish a pattern of inadequate corporate-level attention to care quality.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 5: "The pre-existing conditions defense will block recovery."</h2>

  <p>Elderly plaintiffs invariably have many pre-existing conditions. Defendants invariably argue that the harm was the natural consequence of those conditions, not the alleged abuse. The "egg-shell plaintiff" rule answers this: a defendant takes the victim as found. Tight medical-causation expert work isolates the abuse-caused increment of harm from the baseline. Strong cases rarely turn on this defense once the expert work is done properly.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 6: "The facility will just deny everything in their records."</h2>

  <p>Facility records frequently contradict facility staff testimony, and inconsistencies between charted entries and the medical reality of the situation are powerful evidence. Wound photographs, weight charts, MAR sheets, turn-and-reposition documentation, hospital admission records, and CMS survey results all leave a paper trail. Discrepancies (a chart entry recording a turn that the resident wasn&rsquo;t actually turned for) are particularly damaging to the defense and often support both punitive damages and the heightened-conduct showing for statutory enhancement.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 7: "If the elder has died, the case ends with them."</h2>

  <p>Not in most states, and definitely not in California. The wrongful death statute lets statutorily-defined family members recover for loss of companionship, economic support, and funeral costs. The survival statute lets the estate recover damages the decedent could have recovered if alive. California &sect; 15657 uniquely lets heirs continue to recover the decedent&rsquo;s pre-death pain and suffering &mdash; an enormous departure from the ordinary survival-statute rule. Cases continue and often produce significant recovery after the elder&rsquo;s death.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 8: "I need to wait for the criminal case to finish before I can sue."</h2>

  <p>No. Civil claims and criminal proceedings run on parallel tracks with different parties, different burdens of proof, and different rules. You can file a civil claim while the criminal case is pending. In some situations the criminal proceeding may temporarily stay civil discovery, but the civil claim doesn&rsquo;t wait for criminal completion. In other situations (financial exploitation often), criminal charges are never filed but the civil claim proceeds anyway.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 9: "Attorney fees come out of recovery, so it&rsquo;s really just 60% of the verdict."</h2>

  <p>In ordinary contingency cases, yes. In California &sect; 15657 cases (and similar fee-shifting statutes elsewhere), reasonable attorney fees are recoverable from the defendant in addition to the compensatory damages. The economic structure of the case changes substantially when fees are recoverable from the defendant rather than from the plaintiff&rsquo;s share.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 10: "I&rsquo;d be reporting to APS but I&rsquo;m worried about retaliation."</h2>

  <p>Most states grant broad immunity to good-faith APS reporters. For nursing home residents specifically, federal law (OBRA and the implementing regulations at 42 CFR Part 483) prohibits facility retaliation for reporting, complaining, or being involved in an investigation. Retaliation, if it occurs, becomes its own additional claim and is often a fact a jury responds to strongly. Reports can typically be made anonymously where the reporter is concerned.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 11: "The statute of limitations is long, no rush."</h2>

  <p>Most states impose a 1- to 3-year limitations period on elder abuse and nursing home negligence claims. Wrongful death claims often have a separate (usually 1- to 2-year) period running from the date of death. Some states require pre-suit notice. Financial exploitation claims sometimes benefit from the discovery rule, extending the period. Don&rsquo;t assume you have years. Records are also routinely destroyed under regulatory retention schedules (some as short as 5 years), so early evidence preservation matters separately from the limitations clock.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 12: "Hiring an attorney is expensive."</h2>

  <p>Elder abuse attorneys almost universally work on contingency. No fee unless recovery. Case costs (records, experts, depositions) are advanced by the firm and reimbursed from recovery. In states with fee-shifting statutes (CA &sect; 15657 and others), reasonable fees may be recovered from the defendant in addition to the compensatory damages, further changing the economic picture in the family&rsquo;s favor.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 13: "If the facility is part of a big chain, they&rsquo;ll just settle to make it go away."</h2>

  <p>Sometimes. Large for-profit chains often settle promptly when liability is clear and damages are predictable. But they also have aggressive defense playbooks: shell-corporation structures that shield assets, internal "self-evaluation privilege" assertions that resist discovery, expert witness coordination across multiple cases, and aggressive motion practice to enforce arbitration clauses. The "they&rsquo;ll just settle" assumption is sometimes right and sometimes leads families to undervalue cases. An experienced attorney calibrates expectations to the specific defendant.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 14: "Settlements in elder abuse cases are taxable."</h2>

  <p>Most are not. Compensatory damages for physical injury or physical sickness are excluded from taxable income under IRC &sect; 104(a)(2). Restitution of stolen funds in financial exploitation cases is generally not income (it&rsquo;s the return of the elder&rsquo;s own money). Damages for emotional distress not linked to physical injury, punitive damages, and certain lost-wages components may be taxable. Consult a tax professional for the specific structure of any settlement.</p>

  <h2>Misconception 15: "The case is too small to take seriously."</h2>

  <p>Even seemingly small cases can recover meaningfully under the right state framework. Financial exploitation of $50K may produce $150K in recovery with treble damages and pre-judgment interest. A Stage III pressure ulcer that doesn&rsquo;t kill the elder may still produce a $500K&ndash;$1M settlement with fee-shifting in a strong-statute state. The way to evaluate a case is not to look at the size of the dollars at stake but to look at the statutory framework and the documentary record together. Most experienced elder abuse attorneys offer a free initial consultation and will tell you within an hour whether the case is worth pursuing.</p>

